00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:36,000
Now, last week,

236
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:39,000
in part one of this two-part series,

237
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:41,000
we began discussing

238
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:43,000
difficult behavior in Scouts.

239
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:44,000
Just to catch everybody out,

240
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:45,000
there are times when Scouts

241
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:47,000
just kind of lose their way

242
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:48,000
and they aren't aware

243
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:49,000
of what's happening around them.

244
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:50,000
They're kind of just walking

245
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:51,000
underwater,

246
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:53,000
And anybody who's been around a group

247
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:54,000
of Scouts on a Sunday morning

248
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:56,000
during a weekend camping trip

249
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:58,000
knows exactly what I'm talking about.

250
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:00,000
We know that Scouts sometimes

251
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:01,000
unknowingly misbehaved.

252
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,000
They're not aware of the rules

253
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:04,000
or the right way to do things

254
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:06,000
And they just kind of charge ahead

255
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:07,000
and all that exuberance

256
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:08,000
and energy that they have.

257
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:10,000
We know that Scouts sometimes

258
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:11,000
deliberately misbehaved,

259
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:13,000
That they are aware of the rules

260
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:14,000
or the right things to do,

261
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:16,000
but they make a bad choice

262
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:18,000
And we know that every once in a while

263
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:19,000
a Scout does something

264
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:21,000
that is so just

265
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:23,000
unaccountably foolish and reckless.

266
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:25,000
We find ourselves asking

267
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:27,000
how such an intelligent kid

268
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:29,000
could do something so stupid.

269
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:31,000
Now we also discussed last week

270
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:33,000
why they act this way.

271
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:34,000
We know that physically

272
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:36,000
our Scouts- especially when they're camping,

273
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:37,000
they need plenty of sleep

274
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:38,000
and plenty of food

275
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:39,000
and plenty of water,

276
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:41,000
And I would never, ever discount

277
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:42,000
those physical factors

278
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:44,000
as the source of difficult behavior.

279
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:45,000
Those are a lot of times

280
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:47,000
kind of easy to fix.

281
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:48,000
We know that mentally

282
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:50,000
they need some expectations

283
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:51,000
and they need some boundaries

284
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,000
and they need to stay engaged-

285
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:55,000
And that's always a challenge.

286
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:56,000
And we know that spiritually,

287
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:57,000
they need to develop

288
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:59,000
an internal standard of behavior

289
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:01,000
that guides their actions,

290
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:03,000
And much of our work as Scouts

291
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:05,000
is aimed at forming

292
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:06,000
and maturing

293
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:08,000
that internal standard of behavior.

294
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:10,000
Now, any experienced Scouter

295
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:12,000
or parent will tell you

296
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:14,000
that asking adolescents

297
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:15,000
to contemplate the trade-offs

298
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,000
between risks and benefits

299
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:20,000
is not going to be all that effective

300
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:22,000
in a lot of instances,

301
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:24,000
because we know that their

302
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:26,000
decision-making capabilities

303
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:27,000
are not yet developed

304
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:29,000
And we're working hard

305
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:31,000
to help them develop them right.

306
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:32,000
But sometimes

307
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:34,000
just this kind of logical statement

308
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:35,000
of the facts

309
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:36,000
and reasoning that adults

310
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:38,000
try to impose on young people

311
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:40,000
is not going to help

312
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:42,000
Because, as we learned last week,

313
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:44,000
the decision-making in those

314
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:46,000
adolescent brains is skewed

315
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,000
towards overestimating the benefits

316
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:50,000
of an action

317
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:52,000
and underestimating bad outcomes

318
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:54,000
If they view that some kind

319
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:56,000
of action or decision is going to

320
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:58,000
make them look cool

321
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:00,000
is going to improve their status

322
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:02,000
in the minds of their peers.

323
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:04,000
that's a very, very powerful benefit

324
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:05,000
that they perceive,

325
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:06,000
And sometimes

326
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:08,000
no amount of rational thinking

327
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:09,000
is going to overcome that,

328
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:10,000
Because what seems rational

329
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:12,000
and well-regioned to us

330
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:14,000
is not going to have the same effect

331
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:15,000
on them.

332
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,000
Now, experience is a good teacher,

333
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:19,000
but experience based on negative

334
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
results is not quite

335
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:23,000
as effective in

336
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,000
children and younger adolescents

337
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:27,000
as it is in older adolescents

338
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:28,000
and adults.

339
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:30,000
These things all kind of converge

340
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:32,000
and make risk-taking

341
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:34,000
very attractive to adolescent boys.

342
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:36,000
Remember that you're not dealing

343
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:38,000
with a fully-formed, rational

344
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:39,000
adult.

345
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
So how do we help

346
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
our scouts make

347
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
better decisions when it comes to

348
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
risky or difficult behavior?

349
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,000
Well, risky and difficult behavior

350
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:51,000
is going to have less appeal

351
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:53,000
if scouts see a direct benefit

352
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:55,000
from the alternative,

353
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:57,000
safer courses of action.

354
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:59,000
The benefits of choices in their minds

355
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
outweigh the negative consequences

356
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:02,000
of choices.

357
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:04,000
So we can leverage that

358
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:06,000
by making sure that we put a lot of

359
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:08,000
weight on the benefit of good decisions,

360
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:10,000
And one of the ways we can do that

361
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,000
is make sure that we promote

362
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,000
positive images of good decision-making.

363
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
We can help them build confidence

364
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:18,000
to recognize and reject

365
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:20,000
risky behavior and to get

366
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:22,000
themselves out of risky situations.

367
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,000
Our monitoring and supervision

368
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,000
is going to limit

369
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:28,000
exposure to risky situations,

370
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:30,000
And that's a challenge in scouting,

371
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,000
because monitoring is supervision,

372
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:34,000
so that real responsibility is present

373
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:36,000
on the part of the scouts and

374
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,000
real choices are able to be made.

375
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:40,000
And finally, one very important

376
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:42,000
way we can help

377
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:44,000
our scouts manage risk

378
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:46,000
is making sure that they are occupied

379
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:48,000
with positive activities.

380
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:50,000
I believe in downtime

381
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:52,000
and I believe in sitting around the campfire,

382
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:54,000
kind of hanging out with your pals,

383
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:56,000
And I don't think every single moment of the day needs to be scheduled.

384
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,000
by making positive activities

385
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,000
attractive and

386
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,000
keeping their time filled with them,

387
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:04,000
We really cut back on the possibilities

388
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,000
for poor decision-making.

389
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:08,000
So let's talk about rulemaking

390
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:10,000
for a moment. I've never seen much use

391
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:12,000
in troops establishing elaborate codes of

392
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:14,000
conduct or disciplinary rules

393
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:16,000
and regulations. We already have that.

394
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:18,000
It's built into scouting. We have the

395
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:20,000
scout oath and the scout law.

396
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:22,000
That's our code. If we

397
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:24,000
use the scout oath and the scout law

398
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:26,000
as our rules of conduct,

399
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:28,000
then we are able to

400
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:30,000
deal with scouts individually

401
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:32,000
based on their understanding of the scout oath

402
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:34,000
and the scout law.

403
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:36,000
And we deal with scouts individually

404
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:38,000
based on their understanding-

405
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:40,000
not ours, but their understanding-

406
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:42,000
of the scout oath and law.

407
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:44,000
We counsel with them and we work with them,

408
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:46,000
We build

409
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:48,000
an internal standard of behavior

410
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:50,000
that will not only apply to scouts

411
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:52,000
but will apply to the rest of their lives.

412
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,000
And when it comes to rulemaking,

413
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,000
we also need to remember

414
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:58,000
that scouts are much more motivated

415
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:00,000
by the promise of rewards

416
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:02,000
than they are discouraged by the threat

417
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:04,000
of punishment. Set rules

418
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:06,000
and regulations for punishment,

419
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:08,000
you know, zero tolerance policies

420
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,000
and things like that Prevent us

421
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:12,000
from doing the work that is most important,

422
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:14,000
and that is dealing with scouts

423
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:16,000
on an individual basis

424
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:18,000
and thus building that internal standard.

425
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:20,000
Baden Powell

426
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:22,000
had a couple of things to say about this.

427
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:24,000
He said it's risky to order

428
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:26,000
a boy not to do something.

429
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:28,000
It immediately opens to him the adventure

430
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:30,000
of doing it. He also said

431
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:32,000
the boy is not governed by

432
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,000
don't but is led

433
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:36,000
by do. I think

434
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:38,000
the scout oath and law are

435
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:40,000
powerful tools for positive reinforcement.

436
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:42,000
The only tools we have

437
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:44,000
in our toolbox are positive.

438
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:46,000
Try it. Instead of telling everybody

439
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:48,000
what's prohibited, tell them what's expected.

440
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:50,000
Remember negative reinforcement.

441
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:52,000
once again, it's a weak

442
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:54,000
method for scout age boys

443
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:56,000
and I don't think it's consistent with the values of the scout oath and law.

444
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:58,000
The scout oath and law

445
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:00,000
is not a set of prohibitions,

446
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:02,000
It's a set of aspirations

447
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:04,000
And if we are consistently

448
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:06,000
aiming at the aspirations,

449
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:08,000
the prohibitions take care of themselves.

450
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:10,000
So now we understand

451
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:12,000
something about the sources

452
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:14,000
of difficult behavior. We know

453
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:16,000
some of the causes involved.

454
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,000
We know that we want to use

455
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:20,000
strong, positive reinforcement

456
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:22,000
whenever possible. We know that there are

457
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:24,000
things that we can do to minimize

458
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:26,000
possibility of bad

459
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:28,000
decision making and risky behavior.

460
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:30,000
But you know what's coming next. right,

461
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:32,000
If we get everything

462
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:34,000
right and we're still

463
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:36,000
working very, very hard on

464
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:38,000
all of these fronts, we

465
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:40,000
understand that at some point

466
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:42,000
we're going to be dealing with

467
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:44,000
risky or difficult behavior in our

468
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:46,000
scouts. So how do we

469
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:48,000
react? If we understand

470
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:50,000
the way that things work

471
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:52,000
with scouts, we know

472
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:54,000
that reactive punishment

473
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:56,000
is going to have little effect

474
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:58,000
on their future ability to make

475
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:00,000
better decisions, And that's what

476
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:02,000
we're aimed at: building the internal

477
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:04,000
standards so they can make better

478
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:06,000
decisions. Anger or punishments

479
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:08,000
are weak tools and may

480
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:10,000
even make scouts even more

481
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:12,000
recalcitrant and drive them

482
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:14,000
towards more risky behavior or bad

483
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:16,000
decisions. Now, a boys will

484
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,000
be boys attitude is

485
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,000
absolutely not an option,

486
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,000
But neither is reacting with anger

487
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:24,000
or punishment. A more empathetic

488
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:26,000
and compassionate and useful approach

489
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:28,000
is learning about

490
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:30,000
why they made

491
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:32,000
a bad decision or why

492
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:34,000
they participated in a risky behavior.

493
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:36,000
We do that by learning a little

494
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:38,000
bit more about how

495
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:40,000
they look at the world And then we

496
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:42,000
apply that understanding

497
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:44,000
to helping them manage risky situations

498
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
and reacting rationally

499
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,000
rather than emotionally

500
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,000
when they make bad choices of action.

501
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,000
When we run into problems. we need

502
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:54,000
to differentiate between proportionate

503
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:56,000
consequences

504
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,000
and corrective punishment.

505
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:00,000
This is a really important distinction.

506
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
Proportionate consequences are

507
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,000
the direct result of misbehavior.

508
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,000
So sitting out

509
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:08,000
an activity that you disrupted,

510
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:10,000
or being restricted from using something

511
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
like a knife that was

512
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:14,000
misused, resolving a conflict

513
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,000
caused by bad behavior,

514
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:18,000
cleaning up a mess caused

515
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
by difficult behavior-

516
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:22,000
that's proportionate consequences.

517
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,000
It deals with

518
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:26,000
things that are a direct result

519
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:28,000
of the behavior.

520
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,000
Corrective punishment,

521
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,000
on the other hand, is any

522
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:34,000
sanction beyond

523
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,000
those proportionate consequences

524
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,000
A very important distinction,

525
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:40,000
I am going to say.

526
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:42,000
in my opinion,

527
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
scouters and youth leaders

528
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,000
should never apply

529
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:48,000
corrective punishment.

530
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:50,000
I talked about that a moment ago.

531
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,000
Systems of corrective punishments,

532
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:54,000
such as demerits or forced physical

533
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:56,000
activity like pushups or the like,

534
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:58,000
are completely out of place in scouting.

535
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:00,000
That kind of a system of rules and sanctions

536
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,000
requires enforcement and policing,

537
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:04,000
and scouters and youth leaders

538
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:06,000
are not enforcers

539
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:08,000
and they're not police.

540
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,000
If we meet

541
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:12,000
difficult behavior with constructive discipline

542
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
that determines

543
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
the appropriate consequences,

544
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:18,000
we're going to be much more successful.

545
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:20,000
We need to counsel the scout

546
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:22,000
and discuss the behavior

547
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,000
And in that counseling we separate

548
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
the behavior from the scout.

549
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:28,000
We work together to determine

550
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:30,000
what proportionate consequences there are.

551
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:32,000
Let's talk very specifically

552
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:34,000
about some steps that we

553
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:36,000
would take in response to difficult behavior.

554
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:38,000
Number one:

555
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:40,000
we intervene immediately to stop

556
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,000
anything that is injurious to individuals

557
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:44,000
or damaging to property.

558
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:46,000
We just immediately stop that.

559
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:48,000
Number two is: once we've stopped

560
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
whatever is going on,

561
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:52,000
we have to take the time

562
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
to quiet our own emotional response.

563
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
I know I do and I'm going to

564
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,000
guess that you do too.

565
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,000
Of course, this is really difficult. a lot of the time,

566
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
If we respond emotionally,

567
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:04,000
it's likely scouts are going to view

568
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:06,000
the consequences of their poor decision

569
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,000
as something that upset us,

570
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,000
rather than

571
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,000
the consequences being a direct

572
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,000
result of the decision that they made.

573
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,000
I want to spend a moment talking

574
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,000
about quieting that emotional response.

575
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
A lot of times you're going to need somebody

576
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,000
to help you do that. You're going to need to

577
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,000
grab a fellow scatter and say I am really upset

578
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:26,000
right now and I need you to talk to me

579
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
for a moment until I get over this.

580
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,000
A couple of things that will

581
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
help you think your way through this

582
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
is remember that what's happening

583
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
is part of a developmental process.

584
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
We've all made plenty of

585
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
bad decisions in the past ourselves,

586
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,000
So it's

587
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
important to understand that

588
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:46,000
what's going on is not

589
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
intended to hurt you,

590
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
nor it is indicative of the quality

591
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:52,000
or value of your service. as a

592
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:54,000
scouter. We have to be

593
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
very careful not to take these

594
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
things too personally. So once

595
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
we've stopped whatever was going on

596
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
and quieted our own emotional

597
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
response, we need to start

598
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,000
asking questions. We need to get to

599
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,000
the heart of the matter. We need to

600
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
listen carefully to the answers.

601
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,000
and once we've listened carefully to the answers,

602
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
we can help scouts discover

603
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
the moral and ethical principles

604
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:18,000
that bear on the situation.

605
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,000
We're not going to do this when we're

606
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
emotional. We're not going to do this

607
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
without doing a lot of listening,

608
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,000
And we're not going to do this by preaching

609
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:28,000
to the scouts. We're going to do this by

610
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
asking questions. We're going to reserve

611
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
issuing judgment and we're going to ask

612
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,000
the scouts to judge themselves.

613
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:36,000
That way, we're invoking

614
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:38,000
that developing internal standard of behavior.

615
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:40,000
Here's an example. okay,

616
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:42,000
Let's say a scout on a dare

617
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:44,000
collapses a fellow scout's

618
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:46,000
tent at summer camp.

619
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:48,000
That's going to make me emotional and crazy.

620
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,000
I'm going to make sure first that there's no more

621
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:52,000
injury or damage possible.

622
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:54,000
I'm going to step away. I'm going to take

623
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:56,000
a couple of deep breaths. I'm going to

624
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,000
count to ten. I'm going to talk to one

625
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:00,000
of my fellow scouters. Then I'm going to

626
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:02,000
sit down with the scout and I'm going to ask

627
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,000
the scout to come in there. He's going to tell me

628
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:06,000
something like: well, I knocked John's

629
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,000
tent down. I'm going to say: well,

630
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:10,000
why did you do that? He may be angry

631
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:12,000
and upset and embarrassed at the whole

632
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:14,000
situation. He might be a little recalcitrant.

633
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:16,000
He's going to say: well, I don't know.

634
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:18,000
I'm going to reply. I have a hard time

635
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:20,000
believing that you just suddenly decided

636
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:22,000
that this was a good idea. Tell me

637
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:24,000
what you were thinking. The scout's not

638
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:26,000
going to be happy about talking to me.

639
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,000
He's going to say: I don't know,

640
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:30,000
Nobody suggested this would be a good idea.

641
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:32,000
It came to you on your own and maybe

642
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:34,000
he'll allow that. the other guys may have

643
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:36,000
edged him on a little bit. I'm going to ask

644
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:38,000
him if what he did

645
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,000
turned out to be a very good idea.

646
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:42,000
He's going to say: I don't think so.

647
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:44,000
I'm going to tell him that I agree

648
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:46,000
and I'm going to ask him to think of

649
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:48,000
what points of the scout law

650
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:50,000
he may have forgotten for a moment.

651
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:52,000
and you know he's going to love

652
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:54,000
this.

653
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:56,000
He's going to look at me

654
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:58,000
and he's going to say, well,

655
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:00,000
I don't know, because he doesn't

656
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,000
want to talk about this. You know,

657
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,000
really, You don't know.

658
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,000
No, I don't know. I just kind

659
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,000
of did it. I'll say, all right,

660
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:10,000
well, listen, you need some time to think

661
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,000
about this. if you aren't ready to talk to me now,

662
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:14,000
Why don't you sit right here and wait

663
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:16,000
for a while? I can be back in 15 or 20

664
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:18,000
minutes. Usually, the idea of

665
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:20,000
sitting there and waiting for me to come

666
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:22,000
back is not very attractive.

667
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:24,000
So that might loosen them up a little bit

668
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:26,000
and I'm going to get something like: well,

669
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:28,000
I knew what I was doing was wrong,

670
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:30,000
but it's no big deal.

671
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:32,000
I won't do it again.

672
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:34,000
I'm sorry, Leave me alone.

673
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:36,000
I said: well, you know, it's kind of a big deal.

674
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:38,000
It's a big deal to me, It's a big deal

675
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:40,000
to the guy whose tent you knocked over.

676
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:42,000
Can you understand that? that would be

677
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:44,000
really upsetting.

678
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:46,000
Well, yeah, I kind of understand. Well,

679
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:48,000
how are you going to make it right? Well,

680
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:50,000
I could apologize. Okay,

681
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,000
Is there anything else? Yeah, I can set

682
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:54,000
the tent back up, All right, look,

683
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:56,000
listen to me, I think you got it right.

684
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:58,000
I know the last thing you want to do

685
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:00,000
is be sitting here talking to me about

686
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:02,000
this, but I can't ignore what

687
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:04,000
you're doing. Need to apologize, Need to

688
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:06,000
set the tent back up, But before we're

689
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:08,000
finished, I need you to think about

690
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:10,000
and tell me what part of the scout

691
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:12,000
law you forgot when you did

692
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:14,000
this. Well, maybe I forgot to be

693
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:16,000
trustworthy. Maybe I forgot to be friendly.

694
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:18,000
Maybe I forgot to be kind.

695
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:20,000
Okay, see, that

696
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:22,000
was easy, right, I know you knew that.

697
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:24,000
Listen, we all forgot ourselves

698
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:26,000
once in a while and we do

699
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:28,000
things we regret. Can I

700
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:30,000
trust you to go ahead and make this right

701
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:32,000
and not to repeat it, please?

702
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:34,000
Well, yeah, I suppose. so

703
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:36,000
I knew I could count on you

704
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:38,000
and the next time we talk, let's

705
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:40,000
have something better to talk about. Now,

706
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:42,000
that's an idealized conversation,

707
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:44,000
Okay, and of course,

708
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:46,000
I was perfectly level-headed

709
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:48,000
and consistent all the way

710
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:50,000
through it. It's not always going to

711
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:52,000
happen. I work hard at trying

712
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:54,000
to concentrate:

713
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:56,000
getting the scout to think through

714
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:58,000
the action, getting them to identify

715
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:00,000
what the proportionate consequences

716
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:02,000
of that action ought to be

717
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:04,000
and getting them to analyze

718
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:06,000
their decision in the

719
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:08,000
light of the scout oath and law.

720
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:10,000
A scout who behaves poorly

721
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:12,000
is not going to be real thrilled about getting

722
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:14,000
caught. They're going to be upset

723
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:16,000
and angry. They'll be reacting

724
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:18,000
emotionally. Now, compounding

725
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:20,000
that emotional reaction with

726
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:22,000
my own is the way that we have

727
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:24,000
really ugly scenes

728
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:26,000
and difficulties. If we take

729
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:28,000
the time we quiet the emotional

730
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:30,000
reaction, we don't start

731
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:32,000
issuing judgments, we start

732
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:34,000
asking questions and we

733
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:36,000
work by being empathetic

734
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:38,000
and finding something

735
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:40,000
complimentary to say about the scout,

736
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:42,000
separating him from the behavior.

737
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:44,000
You know I expect more

738
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:46,000
from you. I know I can expect

739
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:48,000
more from you. I know that this was a mistake,

740
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:50,000
so let's not let it happen again.

741
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:52,000
Okay, that can be really challenging,

742
00:24:52,000 --> 00:24:54,000
but if you work at it

743
00:24:54,000 --> 00:24:56,000
you'll make it happen. If we learn

744
00:24:56,000 --> 00:24:58,000
how to be disappointed in

745
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:00,000
behavior without being

746
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:02,000
disappointed in a scout- and that

747
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:04,000
can take some learning- we can

748
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:06,000
start exercising

749
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:08,000
positive reinforcement.

750
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:10,000
We want our scouts to do better

751
00:25:10,000 --> 00:25:12,000
and we don't achieve that

752
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:14,000
by making them feel worse.

753
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:16,000
Bad behavior diminishes

754
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:18,000
if we make a habit of looking

755
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:20,000
for and recognizing

756
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:22,000
good behavior, If we

757
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:24,000
exercise positive reinforcement.

758
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:26,000
a few words of honest

759
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:28,000
praise from somebody you respect

760
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:30,000
are a really powerful force for good.

761
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:32,000
So I know what you're thinking.

762
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:34,000
that all sounds well and good.

763
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:36,000
I'll give it a shot. but what happens

764
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:38,000
if a scout keeps misbehaving?

765
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:40,000
What if that guy you're talking about

766
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:42,000
went ahead and kept on knocking

767
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:44,000
down tents? What if the behavior

768
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:46,000
was very serious and it damaged

769
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:48,000
something or injured someone?

770
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:50,000
Repetitive or serious bad behavior

771
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:52,000
is something that now

772
00:25:52,000 --> 00:25:54,000
we're going to discuss with

773
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:56,000
the scout and with their

774
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:58,000
parent. We're going to go through the

775
00:25:58,000 --> 00:26:00,000
steps I described in:

776
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:02,000
learning about what was going on

777
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:04,000
and finding out what the

778
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:06,000
proportionate consequences are and everything like that.

779
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:08,000
but we're going to leave it up to

780
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:10,000
parents to employ

781
00:26:10,000 --> 00:26:12,000
any kind of corrective punishment.

782
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:14,000
and you know, it's actually

783
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:16,000
pretty simple. We had a little trouble

784
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:18,000
on the camping trip. Your son

785
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:20,000
needs to explain what this trouble was.

786
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:22,000
we have sorted

787
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:24,000
it out as far as we're concerned.

788
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:26,000
but any correction that you feel you need

789
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:28,000
to do at home once you understand

790
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:30,000
what went on

791
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:32,000
would help back us up,

792
00:26:32,000 --> 00:26:34,000
and parents will usually respond pretty positively

793
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:36,000
to that. What happens

794
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:38,000
when we go through those steps

795
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:40,000
and we still have misbehavior?

796
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:42,000
Well, we're going to go back to the parents

797
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:44,000
and we're going to say this behavior

798
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:46,000
hasn't been addressed properly. We're doing our best,

799
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:48,000
we've done everything that we can do,

800
00:26:48,000 --> 00:26:50,000
but it's time for you to step in

801
00:26:50,000 --> 00:26:52,000
and you're going to have to make

802
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:54,000
whatever corrections you're going to need

803
00:26:54,000 --> 00:26:56,000
or your son's not going to be able to continue to participate

804
00:26:56,000 --> 00:26:58,000
until you assure us

805
00:26:58,000 --> 00:27:00,000
that that behavior isn't going to be repeated.

806
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:02,000
So there's this kind of

807
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:04,000
three-tiered approach. A lot of behavior

808
00:27:04,000 --> 00:27:06,000
we can handle. We can handle it at the moment

809
00:27:06,000 --> 00:27:08,000
and we can resolve it.

810
00:27:08,000 --> 00:27:10,000
There can be proportionate consequences to it.

811
00:27:10,000 --> 00:27:12,000
If it's repetitive or really

812
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:14,000
risky or dangerous, parents

813
00:27:14,000 --> 00:27:16,000
need to be involved. They need to

814
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:18,000
impose any corrective punishment

815
00:27:18,000 --> 00:27:20,000
that they feel is appropriate.

816
00:27:20,000 --> 00:27:22,000
If, after those two steps,

817
00:27:22,000 --> 00:27:24,000
we're still having a behavior problem,

818
00:27:24,000 --> 00:27:26,000
it's very plain

819
00:27:26,000 --> 00:27:28,000
and it needs to be laid out

820
00:27:28,000 --> 00:27:30,000
to a set of parents. Look, this behavior

821
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:32,000
is still continuing

822
00:27:32,000 --> 00:27:34,000
and until you can assure us

823
00:27:34,000 --> 00:27:36,000
that you've corrected it and your son

824
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:38,000
can come back and participate

825
00:27:38,000 --> 00:27:40,000
with scouts and not continue

826
00:27:40,000 --> 00:27:42,000
to misbehave, he's not going to be able

827
00:27:42,000 --> 00:27:44,000
to participate From right now.

828
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:46,000
until I have your personal assurance

829
00:27:46,000 --> 00:27:48,000
that this behavior isn't going to be repeated,

830
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:50,000
he won't be able to participate in meetings

831
00:27:50,000 --> 00:27:52,000
or campouts. And you know

832
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:54,000
the discussion is probably not going to be quite that simple,

833
00:27:54,000 --> 00:27:56,000
but that's my opinion

834
00:27:56,000 --> 00:27:58,000
and my suggestion of how to proceed

835
00:27:58,000 --> 00:28:00,000
in that kind of extreme situation.

836
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:02,000
So how many times do we come across

837
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:04,000
a situation that extreme?

838
00:28:04,000 --> 00:28:06,000
I will tell you in my personal experience

839
00:28:06,000 --> 00:28:08,000
it's very rare: Not once a year,

840
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:10,000
not once every five years.

841
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:12,000
And the rarity of that is owing to the fact

842
00:28:12,000 --> 00:28:14,000
that we use positive reinforcement,

843
00:28:14,000 --> 00:28:16,000
that we use

844
00:28:16,000 --> 00:28:18,000
the Scout Oath and Law

845
00:28:18,000 --> 00:28:20,000
as our standard of behavior,

846
